  "Santa Barbara's housing stock is really three different cities as far as appliances are concerned. The Riviera and the Upper East run on Spanish Colonial Revival homes from the 1920s and 30s — the rebuild era after the 1925 earthquake — with tight galley kitchens, built-in refrigeration retrofitted decades after the walls went up, and electrical panels that were never sized for a modern kitchen. The Mesa is postwar ranch construction with the Pacific a few blocks away. San Roque and Samarkand sit in between — mid-century homes with a mix of original and updated kitchens. Each one fails differently, and we work all three every week.",
  "The estate side of our Santa Barbara work — Hope Ranch, the upper Riviera, and next door in <a href=\"/montecito/\">Montecito</a> — runs on built-in Sub-Zero refrigeration, Wolf and Viking ranges, Miele dishwashers, wine rooms, and full outdoor kitchens. Most repair outfits on the South Coast service some of that list. Almost none service all of it, and nearly nobody touches the outdoor side.",
  `Diagnostic fee: <strong>$89</strong> — waived with repair. Written estimate before we start. <strong>90-day warranty</strong> on parts and labor. Call <a href="tel:${phoneRaw}">${branch.phone}</a> for same-day service across Santa Barbara — and the rest of the <a href="/santa-barbara-county/">South Coast corridor</a>.`,
  "<strong>From our techs on coastal Santa Barbara:</strong> if you live on the Mesa, near West Beach, or anywhere within a mile of the water — have your refrigerator's condenser coils cleaned every 4–6 months. Salt air deposits on coil fins faster than inland dust ever will, the compressor works harder to shed heat, and the failure you eventually pay for is the expensive one. We check coils on every Santa Barbara refrigerator call as standard, not as an add-on."
